ow is **from my own knowledge**, not a database query. The identifiers I give are recalled standard identifiers — easy to verify, but I did not look them up in this session.\n\n## The common thread\n\nAll five are core nodes of the **p53 signalling pathway / DNA-damage response (DDR)**, and together they form a near-complete minimal circuit of it:\n\n| Gene | Role in the circuit | Locus | Entrez (recalled) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **ATM** | Upstream sensor kinase; activated by DNA double-strand breaks, phosphorylates p53 (Ser15) and CHK2/MDM2 | 11q22.3 | 472 |\n| **TP53** | The hub — transcription factor, \"guardian of the genome\" | 17p13.1 | 7157 |\n| **MDM2** | E3 ubiquitin ligase that degrades p53 — *and* itself a p53 target gene (negative feedback) | 12q15 | 4193 |\n| **CDKN1A** (p21) | p53 target; CDK inhibitor → G1/S cell-cycle arrest (the \"pause\" arm) | 6p21.2 | 1026 |\n| **BAX** | p53 target; pro-apoptotic BCL-2 family, drives mitochondrial outer-membrane permeabilisation (the \"die\" arm) | 19q13.33 | 581 |\n\nSo the set reads as: **damage sensor (ATM) → hub (TP53) → autoregulatory brake (MDM2) → two divergent effector outputs (CDKN1A = arrest, BAX = apoptosis)**. The MDM2 loop is the classic p53 negative feedback oscillator.\n\nSecondary shared features:\n\n- **Pathway membership:** all five are annotated members of **KEGG hsa04115 (p53 signaling pathway)**. TP53/MDM2/CDKN1A/BAX also sit in Reactome **R-HSA-3700989 (Transcriptional Regulation by TP53)**; ATM enters via Reactome **R-HSA-6804756 (Regulation of TP53 Activity through Phosphorylation)**. GO-wise they converge on **GO:0072331, signal transduction by p53 class mediator**.\n- **Cancer genetics:** TP53 and ATM are tumour suppressors with germline syndromes — **Li-Fraumeni** (TP53) and **ataxia-telangiectasia** (biallelic ATM), with heterozygous ATM a moderate-penetrance breast/pancreatic cancer risk allele. MDM2 is the **oncogene** of the group (amplified in sarcomas/glioma). CDKN1A and BAX are rarely mutated but frequently dysregulated downstream.\n- **Druggability:** this is the pathway targeted by **MDM2–p53 interaction inhibitors** (nutlin class: idasanutlin, navtemadlin) and **ATM/ATR inhibitors** used for synthetic-lethal strategies with PARP inhibition.\n\nOne caveat on interpretation: because this set is so tightly co-annotated, it is exactly the kind of gene list that will return p53 signalling as the top hit in any enrichment test — which is informative if the list came from an unbiased screen, but circular if it was assembled from a p53 pathway annotation in the first place. Worth knowing which it was before you report enrichment as a finding.\n\nIf you want this computed rather than recalled, the usual route is a g:Profiler / Enrichr / DAVID over-representation test against the appropriate background, or STRING for the interaction graph — none of which I can reach from this session.\n\n## ANSWER\n\nAll five are core components of the **p53 signalling / DNA-damage-response pathway** (KEGG hsa04115), forming a minimal functional circuit: **ATM** (DSB sensor kinase) activates **TP53** (hub transcription factor), which induces **MDM2** (E3 ligase, negative-feedback repressor of p53), **CDKN1A**/p21 (cell-cycle arrest arm), and **BAX** (apoptosis arm). All are cancer-associated: TP53/ATM tumour suppressors (Li-Fraumeni; ataxia-telangiectasia), MDM2 an oncogene.\n\n*Source: my own knowledge — the available Galaxy toolset has no gene-annotation or enrichment lookup, so no value here was computed or looked up.*","ttft_ms":1854,"ttft_stream_ms":1113,"time_to_request_ms":23,"type":"result","duration_ms":48592,"uuid":"06a57800-d80b-4449-bf59-5ad7be4ff264"}